WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.890
Same quality every batch, honest labels, and true costs.

2
00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:08.890
This is the video for module three of Basic Baking and Snacks Production.

3
00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:22.600
A quality standard is a short description of what good looks, feels and tastes like, so you can check every batch. Most fried-snack faults come from oil that is too cool, or an overcrowded pot.

4
00:00:23.850 --> 00:00:29.800
A soggy meat pie base usually means hot or runny filling. Cool and thicken the filling first.

5
00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:42.230
A cake that sinks usually means the door was opened early, or it was underbaked. Keep the door shut and test with a skewer. Change only one cause per batch, and write it down.

6
00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:56.770
Before any batch leaves your kitchen, check five things. Even colour. Fully cooked inside. The same size and weight, because customers judge value by size. A taste test. And cooled before packing.

7
00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:12.120
For example, Rukayat in Ilorin finds about one in five chin chin pieces pale inside. She fried bigger batches to save time, so the oil cooled. She sends only the good pieces and explains honestly to the shop.

8
00:01:13.370 --> 00:01:24.870
An allergen can cause a serious reaction in some people, from a rash to difficulty breathing. You cannot see who is allergic, so declare them. Wheat is in all five products.

9
00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:29.360
Eggs are in chin chin, the pastry egg wash and cake.

10
00:01:30.610 --> 00:01:33.410
Milk is in chin chin, butter and many cakes.

11
00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:38.280
Soy can be in some margarines and oils. Check what you buy.

12
00:01:39.530 --> 00:01:48.710
And groundnuts, if you add them or share oil or equipment with nut snacks. Never write gluten-free, sugar-free or healthy for diabetics.

13
00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:58.920
For example, Uche in Enugu packs chin chin in 150 g pouches. The name says what it really is: fried sweet wheat snack.

14
00:02:00.170 --> 00:02:06.860
Ingredients are listed heaviest first, and allergens are marked: wheat, egg, milk, and may contain soy.

15
00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:12.020
Net weight, in grams. Never sell a pack below the weight printed.

16
00:02:13.270 --> 00:02:25.830
Date made and expiry date, written in full, and a batch number. She sets the expiry from her own test. Seal packs, open one each week, and note when smell, taste or crunch changes.

17
00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:41.250
Her business name, full address and phone number. Shop products also need NAFDAC registration. Check the current rules with NAFDAC directly. And refuse any bread improver with potassium bromate. It is banned.

18
00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:55.090
Many sellers work hard and have no money at month end, because gas, oil and packaging are forgotten. For example, Uche costs one batch of chin chin. These are example prices only.

19
00:02:56.340 --> 00:03:01.730
Ingredients, including a share of frying oil, are about 3,100 naira.

20
00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:06.200
Gas for mixing and frying, about 400 naira.

21
00:03:07.450 --> 00:03:12.280
Ten pouches and ten printed labels, about 1,500 naira.

22
00:03:13.530 --> 00:03:27.140
The full batch costs about 5,000 naira. Divided by ten pouches, that is about 500 naira each. She adds 100 naira per pouch to pay herself, so her true cost is about 600 naira.

23
00:03:28.390 --> 00:03:31.480
Her true cost is about 600 naira per pouch.

24
00:03:32.730 --> 00:03:36.970
Similar chin chin nearby sells for 800 to 1,000 naira.

25
00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:45.160
So 900 naira works. If she sold at 600, she would only be paying herself, with nothing to grow.

26
00:03:46.410 --> 00:03:58.330
At 900, she makes about 300 naira profit, a margin of about 33%. Shops buy at a lower price, so work out your wholesale price from your true cost first.

27
00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:09.780
Each week, write four numbers: units sold, waste, profit and repeat customers. After a month you will see which product earns most and where waste eats your money.

28
00:04:10.130 --> 00:04:19.730
Keep business money separate from household money. And when flour goes up, tell regular customers and raise the price. Do not quietly shrink the size.

29
00:04:20.980 --> 00:04:32.240
Your practice: write quality standards for two products, draft a full label, start a four-week shelf-life test, and cost one real batch with retail and wholesale prices.

30
00:04:32.590 --> 00:04:34.750
Now continue with the lessons below.
